WHY ENCRYPTION EXISTS
│
├── Purpose of Encryption
│   ├── Protection Goal
│   │   └── Protects the readability and meaning of data when someone
│   │       obtains it without the required cryptographic authority.
│   ├── What It Does Not Guarantee
│   │   └── Data may still be copied or stolen; encryption aims to keep
│   │       the obtained data unreadable.
│   └── Defense in Depth
│       └── Encryption works alongside authorization, secure application
│           handling, logging, and monitoring rather than replacing them.
│
├── Encryption Terminology
│   ├── Plaintext
│   │   └── Original readable data.
│   ├── Ciphertext
│   │   └── Data transformed into an encrypted, unreadable form.
│   ├── Cryptographic Key
│   │   └── Cryptographic material used as part of encryption or
│   │       decryption operations.
│   ├── Encryption
│   │   └── Converts plaintext into ciphertext.
│   └── Decryption
│       └── Converts ciphertext back into plaintext through an authorized
│           decryption path.
│
├── Basic Data Transformation
│   ├── Encryption Flow
│   │   └── Plaintext → Encryption → Ciphertext
│   └── Decryption Flow
│       └── Ciphertext → Authorized decryption → Plaintext
│
├── Authorization and Encryption
│   ├── Authorization
│   │   ├── Question Answered
│   │   │   └── Can this identity perform this action on this resource?
│   │   └── Example
│   │       └── Whether a Lambda execution role may retrieve an S3 object.
│   ├── Encryption
│   │   ├── Question Answered
│   │   │   └── Can these bytes be understood without the required key
│   │   │       access?
│   │   └── Protection Boundary
│   │       └── Protects data readability rather than deciding every
│   │           permitted resource operation.
│   └── Critical Distinction
│       ├── Authorization protects access to the resource.
│       ├── Encryption protects the meaning and readability of the data.
│       └── Secure systems commonly require both controls.
│
├── Resource Access and Key Usage
│   ├── Resource Permission
│   │   └── Determines whether the principal may access the encrypted
│   │       resource, such as retrieving an S3 object.
│   ├── Key Permission
│   │   └── Determines whether the principal may use the applicable key
│   │       through a permitted cryptographic operation.
│   └── Key Rules
│       ├── Resource access is not the same as key usage.
│       ├── Key usage is not the same as resource access.
│       ├── Access to an encrypted resource does not always guarantee
│       │   access to usable plaintext.
│       └── Permission related to a key does not automatically grant
│           access to every resource protected by that key.
│
├── Data States
│   ├── Data at Rest
│   │   ├── Definition
│   │   │   └── Data stored somewhere.
│   │   └── Examples
│   │       └── S3 objects, database records, EBS volumes, backups, and
│   │           snapshots.
│   ├── Data in Transit
│   │   ├── Definition
│   │   │   └── Data moving between systems.
│   │   └── Examples
│   │       └── Browser to API Gateway, Lambda to another API, or an
│   │           application to a database.
│   └── Data in Use
│       ├── Definition
│       │   └── Data currently being processed.
│       └── Examples
│           └── Decrypted information in application memory or a secret
│               temporarily loaded by an application.
│
├── Plaintext Exposure Boundary
│   ├── Authorized Use
│   │   └── An authorized application may decrypt protected information
│   │       because it needs plaintext for processing.
│   ├── Security Question
│   │   └── Where is plaintext allowed to exist, and who is allowed to
│   │       cause decryption?
│   ├── Application Responsibility
│   │   └── After decryption, the application must protect plaintext in
│   │       memory, logs, errors, caches, and exports.
│   └── Key Limitation
│       └── Encryption does not protect a new plaintext copy merely
│           because the original source was encrypted.
│
├── Validated S3 and Lambda Scenario
│   ├── Initial State
│   │   └── Customer records are stored encrypted in S3.
│   ├── Authorized Processing
│   │   └── Lambda is authorized to retrieve and decrypt the records.
│   ├── Exposure
│   │   └── Lambda writes the decrypted customer record into CloudWatch
│   │       Logs as plaintext.
│   ├── Failed Control
│   │   └── Application handling failed.
│   └── Controls That Did Not Fail
│       ├── S3 storage encryption worked correctly.
│       └── Key authorization worked as intended because Lambda was
│           authorized to decrypt the data.
│
├── Authentication and Business Authorization
│   ├── Authentication
│   │   └── Establishes who is making the request.
│   ├── Authorization
│   │   └── Establishes what actions the identity may perform.
│   ├── Encryption
│   │   └── Controls whether protected bytes can become readable.
│   └── Important Rule
│       └── Successful decryption does not prove that the application user
│           owns the record or that the business operation is authorized.
│
├── Corrected Decryption Model
│   ├── Unsafe Assumption
│   │   └── Do not always assume that the application directly receives a
│   │       decryption key and decrypts the data itself.
│   ├── Reason
│   │   └── The exact process depends on whether encryption is server-side
│   │       or client-side.
│   └── Valid Current Mental Model
│       └── Encrypted resource access + authorized decryption path →
│           plaintext becomes available → application must handle it
│           securely.
│
├── Common Misconceptions
│   ├── Encryption prevents data theft
│   │   └── Data can still be copied; encryption protects its readability.
│   ├── Encryption removes the need for authorization
│   │   └── Authorization and encryption protect different boundaries.
│   ├── IAM denial makes encryption unnecessary
│   │   └── Encryption adds protection when data is exposed through
│   │       another path.
│   ├── Encrypted data never becomes plaintext
│   │   └── Applications normally decrypt data before processing it.
│   ├── Storage encryption protects plaintext logs
│   │   └── Plaintext copied to logs requires separate protection.
│   └── Decryption proves correct business access
│       └── Decryption authority does not validate record ownership or
│           application-level authorization.
│
└── Exam Reasoning
    ├── Identify the sensitive data.
    ├── Determine whether it is at rest, in transit, or in use.
    ├── Identify who can access the resource.
    ├── Identify who can use the key or decryption path.
    ├── Locate where ciphertext becomes plaintext.
    └── Examine how plaintext is handled after decryption.